# Prefix `not` as the operand of `==` hides a chained comparison (M1-19).
mode: syntax
---- query
T | where a == not b == c
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..26
  PIPELINE@0..25
    NAME_REF@0..1
      IDENT@0..1 "T"
    WHITESPACE@1..2 " "
    PIPE@2..3 "|"
    WHITESPACE@3..4 " "
    WHERE_OP@4..25
      IDENT@4..9 "where"
      WHITESPACE@9..10 " "
      BIN_EXPR@10..25
        NAME_REF@10..11
          IDENT@10..11 "a"
        WHITESPACE@11..12 " "
        EQ2@12..14 "=="
        WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
        PREFIX_EXPR@15..25
          NOT_KW@15..18 "not"
          WHITESPACE@18..19 " "
          BIN_EXPR@19..25
            NAME_REF@19..20
              IDENT@19..20 "b"
            WHITESPACE@20..21 " "
            EQ2@21..23 "=="
            WHITESPACE@23..24 " "
            NAME_REF@24..25
              IDENT@24..25 "c"
  WHITESPACE@25..26 "\n"

error[DS0114]: `not` needs parentheses here
  --> query:1:16
  |
1 | T | where a == not b == c
  |                ^^^ `not` binds more loosely than `==`
  |
  = help: write it as a call to say what it negates: `not(b == c)`
  = note: without parentheses, `not` negates everything up to the next `and` or `or`
